Yes. **You can store the file checkpoint in PostgreSQL**, but preserving your exactly-once requirement means changing the architecture—not simply replacing the Kafka checkpoint write with a SQL update.

My recommendation is:

> **Atomically save the JSON events and the checkpoint in PostgreSQL, then publish the events using a transactional outbox and Debezium.**

## 1. Why a direct PostgreSQL checkpoint is not enough

This approach is unsafe:

```java
sendRecordsToKafka();
saveCheckpointToPostgres();
```

If Kafka commits and the application crashes before PostgreSQL commits, the application resends those records after restarting.

Reversing the order risks skipping records instead.

Spring can synchronize database and Kafka transactions, but they still commit separately; Spring explicitly documents the possibility that the second commit fails after the first succeeds. **`@Transactional` does not make PostgreSQL and Kafka one atomic transaction.** ([docs.spring.io](https://docs.spring.io/spring-kafka/reference/kafka/transactions.html))

## 2. Recommended architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    F["Immutable CSV files"] --> S["Spring Boot reader"]

    subgraph PG["One PostgreSQL transaction"]
        O["Insert JSON events into outbox"]
        C["Update file checkpoint"]
    end

    S --> O
    S --> C
    O --> D["Debezium + Kafka Connect"]
    D --> K["Kafka topic"]
```

Your application now does this:

```text
Read CSV batch
    ↓
BEGIN PostgreSQL transaction
    Insert JSON events into outbox
    Update that file's checkpoint
COMMIT
```

It **does not send directly to Kafka**. Debezium handles publication from the outbox. This is the purpose of the transactional outbox pattern: keeping database state and the events representing that state consistent. ([debezium.io](https://debezium.io/documentation/reference/stable/transformations/outbox-event-router.html))

### Exactly-once boundaries

| Boundary | Required mechanism |
|---|---|
| CSV → PostgreSQL | Outbox inserts and checkpoint update in one database transaction |
| PostgreSQL → Kafka | Debezium on Kafka Connect with source exactly-once support enabled |
| Kafka → external side effects | Transactional or idempotent downstream processing |

**An outbox alone is not exactly-once Kafka delivery.** Debezium is ordinarily at-least-once. Its PostgreSQL connector supports participating in Kafka Connect’s source exactly-once mechanism, which must be explicitly enabled. Debezium also documents correctness caveats around the underlying transaction implementation, so validate your deployed versions with failure tests. ([debezium.io](https://debezium.io/documentation/reference/stable/configuration/eos.html))

Your **CSV checkpoint lives in PostgreSQL**. Kafka Connect still keeps its own CDC offsets in Kafka internal topics; these are separate from the filename/record checkpoint. ([kafka.apache.org](https://kafka.apache.org/41/configuration/kafka-connect-configs/))

